OBLIGATION 1: EVEN-GRAPH flip-rank rho_E / rainbow R_E vs TOURNAMENT rho_G/R_G (same cube Q_m, different S_n-quotient)
  n  m |E_n| rho_E ceilE  R_E floorE   |G_n| rho_G  R_G
  3  1     2     1     1    1      1       2     1    1
  4  3     3     2     2    1      1       4     2    2
  5  6     7     6     3    2      2      12     4    3
  6 10    16     9     4    3      4      56     7    5
  covering-excess rho-ceil(log2): compare even-graph vs tournament (is it self-dual under the bijection?)
  DIAGNOSIS -- even-graph class orbit sizes in the fund-cycle cube (why even graphs resist subcubes):
    n=5: 7 classes, orbit sizes in Q_6 = [1, 1, 10, 10, 12, 15, 15] (sum=64=2^6); tiny orbits (empty/near-empty even graph) pin the subcube
    n=6: 16 classes, orbit sizes in Q_10 = [1, 6, 10, 15, 15, 20, 45, 60, 60, 60, 72, 90, 90, 120, 180, 180] (sum=1024=2^10); tiny orbits (empty/near-empty even graph) pin the subcube
  => R_E < floor(log2|E_n|) at n=6 (3<4): the tournament law 'rainbow = floor' is NOT self-dual --
     it was SPECIFIC to the tournament quotient (the balanced-cut shape); the SAME cube, quotiented by
     the even-graph S_n-action, packs/covers WORSE. Assumption challenged: flip-rank depends on the QUOTIENT,
     not the cube -- the tournament<->even-graph bijection is a cube-iso but NOT a flip-rank-iso.

